Overview
Across this bounded sample of healthcare education, clinical information systems, nuclear safety and disaster training, organizational culture determines whether staff disclose experience, trust technological representations and act on retained knowledge. Digitally reconfigured dependencies concern new objects of cultural trust—algorithms, data and immersive systems. Persistently established dependencies concern enduring norms of psychological safety, motivation, leadership, professional responsibility and knowledge stewardship.
Digitally reconfigured cultural dependencies
1. Human–machine epistemic trust in clinical AI, knowledge graphs and registries (data and software barrier count 42).
   Trust shifted from relying mainly on instructors and professional documents to judging algorithmic outputs, training data and digital representations. Bias and poor-quality data weakened confidence in AI-supported medical education and federated clinical models (c212;c213). Entity ambiguity constrained the osteosarcoma knowledge graph, while delayed and unreliable registry data encouraged staff to disregard its results (c222;c227). Digital codification therefore retained knowledge only when organizational norms supported validation, error disclosure and critical—not unquestioning—use of machine outputs.
2. Transparency and accountability around digitally governed health knowledge (governance-constraint count 14; formal-control count 8).
   Federated learning and blockchain redistributed responsibility for data, model validation and provenance across participating institutions (c213;c221). These systems promised privacy and tamper-resistant records, but AI education and AR applications still raised confidentiality and data-breach concerns (c212;c213). System trust was conditional on a culture in which responsibility remained visible: staff needed to know who verified knowledge, who could access it and who was accountable when a digital representation was misleading.
3. Psychologically safe familiarization with XR and adaptive systems (usability count 10; digital-capability count 6).
   AIVR and ReviveXR users reported unfamiliarity, dizziness, interface difficulty and variable technical literacy (c214;c216). Disaster-training AR also required technical support and received an unacceptable usability score (c225;c216). These were not solely interface problems. Where difficulty could be admitted without embarrassment and assistance was readily available, staff could develop digital familiarity; where competence was presumed, discomfort and fear of mistakes restricted experiential learning.
Persistently established cultural dependencies
1. Protected learning and reflection within workload-intensive clinical services (workload count 19; human-capacity count 16; scheduling count 4).
   Heavy clinical workloads constrained neuro-oncology education and nurses’ continuing professional development, while registry users lacked time to interpret performance information (c218;c224;c227). Digital access reduced geographical restrictions but did not create organizational permission to learn. Retention remained conditional on a culture that treated training, reflection and knowledge interpretation as legitimate work rather than optional activity.
2. Motivation sustained by leadership follow-through and credible organizational response (adoption-barrier count 16; leadership and incentive counts 1 each).
   Registry personnel disengaged because they expected that analysing or communicating problems would increase their burden without changing the system (c227;c227). Conversely, gamified learning supported MOOC participation, while recognition and revenue sharing encouraged traditional-medicine contributors (c224;c221). Motivation was therefore an established cultural dependency: technology functioned when leaders responded to contributions and recognition was perceived as fair.
3. Relational trust and psychological safety in professional knowledge sharing (cultural-climate count 10).
   Emotional support moderated how occupational stress affected physicians’ online knowledge contributions (c211;c211). Among nursing educators, supportive, trusting and cooperative cultures promoted experience sharing through team discussions, learning platforms and video conferencing (c217;c217). Digital platforms extended the reach of interaction, but they could not compensate for fear, weak collegial trust or reluctance to disclose incomplete knowledge.
4. Professional stewardship and expert responsibility for retained knowledge (human-capacity count 16).
   Experienced instructors remained responsible for programme design and evaluation, while nursing educators, IT specialists and osteosarcoma experts verified digital content and annotation standards (c212;c219;c222). Nuclear-safety material also required expert validation before entering adaptive training (c214;c219). Technology-enabled retention was conditional on a culture that valued specialist judgment and assigned continuing responsibility for knowledge quality.
5. Safety and continuity norms across personnel turnover (process-design count 10; governance count 8).
   Clinical ethics approval, structured curricula and nuclear-safety standards embedded retention within formal professional routines (c209;c212;c214). Such norms became particularly important when experienced personnel departed or Indigenous medical knowledge faced intergenerational loss (c214;c221).
Together, digitally reconfigured dependencies redirect cultural trust toward machines and data, while persistent dependencies sustain willingness to share, learn, validate and act. Technology converts experience into retained organizational knowledge only when both cultural layers operate together.